BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Whose Landscape: The Alps Between Nation and Planet
DTSTART:20261107T000000
DTEND:20261107T000000
DTSTAMP:20261103T185444Z
UID:2cec7fda5625e99a8aca998705b96b@events-near-me
DESCRIPTION:Through conversation and art\, the afternoon invites us to rec
 onsider the Alps not simply as a symbol of Switzerland\, but as a landscap
 e whose meaning\, image\, and future are shared. We will first gather at S
 wissnex for a panel discussion about the notion of ownership and image. As
  noted in the Harvard Art Museums exhibition On Location: Drawing the Outd
 oors \, the “Swiss myth” was partially constructed by non-Swiss Romant
 ics\, such as J. M. W. Turner\, John Ruskin\, and Lord Byron. Two hundred 
 and fifty years later\, the same Alpine landscape is claimed at a larger s
 cale still: as a planetary system of glaciers\, biodiversity corridors\, a
 nd atmospheric records that belong to no one country. Who has ever really 
 owned the image of the Alps—and what role do cultural institutions (muse
